One prerequisite before you commit: The Cisco WAN IP and interface must be confirmed. Do not use `192.168.1.1` as the peer address; that's your upstream gateway, not automatically the Cisco router.

I'll use `CISCO_WAN_IP` below as a placeholder. Replace it with the actual address.

## Final topology and addressing

| Item             | Palo Alto       | Cisco CSR1000v     |
| ---------------- | --------------- | ------------------ |
| WAN interface    | ethernet1/1     | Your WAN interface |
| WAN IP           | 192.168.1.200   | `CISCO_WAN_IP`     |
| Tunnel interface | tunnel.1        | Tunnel1            |
| Tunnel IP        | 10.255.255.1/30 | 10.255.255.2/30    |
| Virtual router   | default         | —                  |
| Security zone    | VPN             | —                  |
| BGP AS           | 65001           | 65000              |
| BGP neighbor     | 10.255.255.2    | 10.255.255.1       |
| Local LAN        | 10.10.10.0/24   | 172.16.50.0/24     |

We'll use tunnel mode, not transport mode. This encrypts the routed data traffic between the two LANs.

## 1. Create the VPN security zone

Navigate to Network → Zones.

1. Click Add.
2. Name: `VPN`
3. Type: `Layer3`
4. Click OK.

We'll attach `tunnel.1` to this zone. Keeping VPN traffic in a separate zone makes security policy enforcement and logging easier.

## 2. Create the tunnel interface

Navigate to Network → Interfaces → Tunnel.

1. Click Add.
2. Enter suffix `1` to create `tunnel.1`.

Under the Config tab:

| Field          | Value                            |
| -------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Virtual Router | `default`                        |
| Security Zone  | `VPN`                            |
| Comment        | `IPsec tunnel to Cisco CSR1000v` |

Under the IPv4 tab:

1. Click Add.
2. Enter `10.255.255.1/30`.

Click OK.

