## 7. Configure BGP on the Palo Alto

Navigate to:

Network → Virtual Routers → default → BGP

Enable BGP.

### General tab

| Field     | Value          |
| --------- | -------------- |
| Enable    | Checked        |
| Router ID | `10.255.255.1` |
| Local AS  | `65001`        |

### Peer Group tab

Add a peer group:

| Field  | Value            |
| ------ | ---------------- |
| Name   | `CSR-EBGP-GROUP` |
| Type   | EBGP             |
| Enable | Checked          |

### Peer tab

Inside the new peer group, add a peer:

| Field                   | Value          |
| ----------------------- | -------------- |
| Name                    | `CSR-PEER`     |
| Enable                  | Checked        |
| Peer AS                 | `65000`        |
| Peer Address            | `10.255.255.2` |
| Local Address Interface | `tunnel.1`     |
| Local Address IP        | `10.255.255.1` |

Keep BGP authentication disabled for the initial build. We can add MD5 authentication after basic peering is established.

### Export rules

We need to advertise the Palo Alto LAN network to Cisco.

Under the BGP export rules, add an export rule:

| Field                | Value           |
| -------------------- | --------------- |
| Name                 | `EXPORT-PA-LAN` |
| Enable               | Checked         |
| Match address prefix | `10.10.10.0/24` |
| Action               | Allow           |

Use the GUI's prefix-list or address-prefix match field, depending on the PAN-OS 11.2.5 screen.

If the GUI requires a prefix list first, create one containing `10.10.10.0/24` and reference it in the export rule.

Important: The Cisco's BGP `network 172.16.50.0 mask 255.255.255.0` statement advertises the Cisco LAN toward Palo Alto. You don't need to redistribute all connected routes on either device.

Click OK to save the BGP configuration.

