## 8. Configure security policies

The tunnel's existence does not automatically authorize LAN traffic. We need security rules.

Navigate to Policies → Security.

Create two rules.

### Rule 1: Palo Alto LAN to Cisco LAN

| Field               | Value               |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Name                | `PA-LAN-TO-CSR-LAN` |
| Source Zone         | `Trust`             |
| Source Address      | `10.10.10.0/24`     |
| Destination Zone    | `VPN`               |
| Destination Address | `172.16.50.0/24`    |
| Application         | `any` initially     |
| Service             | `any`               |
| Action              | Allow               |
| Log at Session End  | Enabled             |

### Rule 2: Cisco LAN to Palo Alto LAN

| Field               | Value               |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Name                | `CSR-LAN-TO-PA-LAN` |
| Source Zone         | `VPN`               |
| Source Address      | `172.16.50.0/24`    |
| Destination Zone    | `Trust`             |
| Destination Address | `10.10.10.0/24`     |
| Application         | `any` initially     |
| Service             | `any`               |
| Action              | Allow               |
| Log at Session End  | Enabled             |

Place these rules above any general deny rules.

We'll narrow the allowed applications and services once connectivity works.

2. Critical PAN-OS BGP Detail: Redistribution Profile


In PAN-OS, adding an Export Rule alone will not advertise a directly connected subnet (10.10.10.0/24) into BGP unless it is first injected into the Local RIB via a Redistribution Rule.
• Create Redistribution Profile: Go to Network → Virtual Routers → default → Redistribution Profiles → IPv4, click Add, name it REDIST-CONNECTED-LAN, check Enable, select Source Type Connect, and filter by interface or prefix 10.10.10.0/24.
• Attach to BGP: Go to Network → Virtual Routers → default → BGP → Redist Rules, click Add, and select REDIST-CONNECTED-LAN. Once it is in the BGP table, your EXPORT-PA-LAN rule will advertise it to the Cisco router.
## 9. Commit

Before committing, check that:

- `tunnel.1` belongs to zone `VPN`.
- `tunnel.1` belongs to virtual router `default`.
- Its IP is `10.255.255.1/30`.
- The IKE Gateway references `ethernet1/1`.
- The IPsec tunnel references `tunnel.1`.
- BGP local AS is `65001`.
- BGP peer AS is `65000`.
- The BGP peer address is `10.255.255.2`.
- The Cisco WAN peer address is correct.

Click Commit.

## 10. Verification after commit

Once the Cisco side is configured and reachable, open the Palo Alto CLI.

Run:

```
show vpn ike-sa
```

Expected: an established IKEv2 Security Association.

```
show vpn ipsec-sa
```

Expected: an active IPsec SA with packet counters.

```
show interface tunnel.1
```

Expected: tunnel interface information.

```
show routing protocol bgp summary
```

Expected: the Cisco peer `10.255.255.2` established and one or more prefixes received.

```
show routing route
```

Expected: a route for `172.16.50.0/24` through `tunnel.1`.

The official Palo Alto configuration sequence is tunnel interface, IKE Gateway, IPsec tunnel, routing, and security policy.&#x20;

